-- title:
--   Words built from congruent copies realize every pair of exponent-sum-zero words on two coordinates
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be the real inner product space of finitely supported real sequences $(x_0,x_1,x_2,\dots)$ with $\langle x,y\rangle=\sum_nx_ny_n$. Let $F(E)$ be the free group whose generators are the points of $E$ (write $[x]$ for the generator of $x\in E$), and let $K\subseteq F(E)$ be the subgroup of words of exponent sum $0$, i.e. the kernel of the homomorphism $F(E)\to\mathbb Z$ sending every generator to $1$.
--
--   Let $\iota$ be an index set and $a:\iota\to E$ an injective configuration. A configuration $b:\iota\to E$ is a *congruent copy* of $a$ if $|b_i-b_j|=|a_i-a_j|$ for all $i,j$. Let $M_a\subseteq F(E)^\iota$ be the submonoid (under coordinatewise multiplication) generated by
--
--   - the constant tuples $(g)_{i\in\iota}$, $g\in F(E)$, and
--   - the copy tuples $([b_i])_{i\in\iota}$, where $b$ ranges over congruent copies of $a$.
--
--   **Theorem.** For all distinct indices $i\ne j$ and all words $g,h\in K$ there is $z\in M_a$ with
--   $$z_i=g\qquad\text{and}\qquad z_j=h .$$
--
--   Thus, on any two coordinates, products of constant tuples and congruent copies of $a$ realize every pair of exponent-sum-zero words; this is the pair-coverage hypothesis, with $N=K$, of the derived-series argument for single-coordinate fractions. Injectivity of $a$ guarantees that every distance $|a_i-a_j|$ with $i\ne j$ is positive.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $E$ is `FiniteEuclidean` (`ℕ →₀ ℝ` with this inner product), $F(E)$ is `FreeGroup FiniteEuclidean`, $K$ is `AugKernel FiniteEuclidean`, and $M_a$ is `(unitGraded a).monoid`: the tuples realized with some cost by the inductive predicate `UnitWords a` (constant tuples with cost $0$, copy tuples with cost $1$, costs adding under products). The conclusion is `PairCoverage (unitGraded a).monoid (AugKernel FiniteEuclidean)`.
-- source:
